## GitHub workflow

- All work is tracked in GitHub issues; issues are the queue.
- Start a session by reading assigned issues: `gh issue list --label "agent:<you>"`.
- Branch per issue: `git checkout -b feat/<issue#>-<slug>`; PR with `closes #<issue>`.
- Never commit to `{{DEFAULT_BRANCH}}` directly. The reviewer agent signs off before merge.
- See the harness plugin's `rules/operating-model.md` for granularity, review-and-fix,
  and session-lifecycle policy.

## Agent roles

- **wave-lead** (harness plugin) — orchestrates one delegated wave end-to-end so the
  owner-facing lead session stays thin.
{{BUILDER_ROSTER}}
- **reviewer** (harness plugin) — review-and-fix: applies mechanical fixes for its own
  findings directly on the PR branch, test-first; routes back to a builder only
  design-level rework. Enforces this file, tests, and lint before merge.
- **researcher** (harness plugin) — investigates unfamiliar APIs/libraries; writes
  findings to `docs/research/`.

## Models

- **Lead / driver session**: a high-level orchestrator only — owner interaction, queue
  triage, checkpoints. Delegates wave orchestration to fresh **wave-lead** subagents
  rather than running waves itself.
- **Builders**: {{BUILDER_MODEL}}, high reasoning effort.
- **Reviewer**: {{REVIEWER_MODEL}} — bug-finding is its edge, and it's the safety net
  for builder output.
- **Researcher**: {{RESEARCHER_MODEL}}.
- Token efficiency is a project goal in its own right — see the harness plugin's
  `rules/token-efficiency.md`; every agent brief should cite it.
<!-- The harness plugin's own rules (operating-model.md, token-efficiency.md) live inside
     the installed plugin, not this repo. Harness skills/agents get the absolute path
     substituted for them; from a plain session, locate them with
     `ls -d ~/.claude/plugins/cache/*/harness/*/rules` (or the marketplace source
     directory, if the plugin was installed from a local path). -->
- **Set every subagent's model explicitly at launch** — the parent session's model does
  not propagate to an overridden subagent.

